# =========================================================
# Copy this file to .env.local for local dev (Next.js reads .env.local)
# AND to .env (the Prisma CLI only auto-loads .env, not .env.local).
# Copy to .env on the VPS for production.
#
# ⚠ This file must list EVERY process.env reference in the codebase. Salon
# shipped to production twice with a var missing from its runbook because the
# list was written from the plan instead of from the code. The authoritative
# inventory is:
#   grep -rn "process.env" app/ lib/ i18n/ middleware.ts next.config.ts scripts/
#
# ⚠ One deliberate omission: NODE_ENV. It is set by the Next.js runtime itself
# (`next dev` → development, `next start` → production, always, and it cannot be
# overridden from a .env file), never by an operator. lib/session.ts and
# scripts/mint-test-session.ts read it to compute the session cookie name — they
# must agree with portal-web, which derives it the same way. Adding it here
# would invite someone to "fix" it and silently break session decoding.
# =========================================================

# =========================================================
# Database (PostgreSQL) — Food owns its own DB (architecture Part C)
# =========================================================
# Local dev: docker-compose Postgres on host port 5434. Taken elsewhere on this
# machine: 5432 Beauty-Salon, 5433 Salon, 5435 Apparel.
# Prod DB is `apoyo_food` with its own role — see Slice 6. ⚠ Percent-encode any
# password containing URL-special characters (APOYO_ECOSYSTEM.md).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://food:food_dev_password@localhost:5434/apoyo_food_dev

# =========================================================
# Assets — PROD ONLY. Prevents /_next collisions when this app is reached via
# portal.apoyolime.com/food/* (architecture Part B2 / F1). That host is shared
# with portal-web, the Apoyo-Demia app, salon-web and apparel-web. Leave unset
# in dev, where one origin serves both surfaces.
# =========================================================
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ASSET_HOST=https://food.apoyolime.com

# =========================================================
# Shared identity (Slice 3). Food NEVER issues a session — it only validates the
# JWT cookie portal-web sets on .apoyolime.com. portal-web is the CONFIRMED sole
# issuer (resolved 2026-07-30; re-verified at the source in lib/session.ts).
# =========================================================
# Must be byte-identical to portal-web's AUTH_SECRET, or every session decodes as
# signed-out. Retrieve from portal-web's VPS .env at deploy time.
# ⚠ It is not merely a lookup miss when this is wrong: portal-web passes
# `salt: sessionCookieName`, and next-auth v5 derives the JWE key from
# (secret, salt) — a mismatch makes the token undecryptable, silently.
AUTH_SECRET=
# Documents the cookie scope Food reads from; this app sets no cookies of its
# own. Prod: .apoyolime.com — dev: .lvh.me
COOKIE_DOMAIN=.apoyolime.com

# =========================================================
# Ecosystem API (Slice 3) — memberships + the §6b registration-config toggle.
# ⚠ /memberships and /users/{id}/memberships are served by BOTH the Apoyo-Demia
# app and portal-web (same identity DB; portal's nginx path-split hides which
# answers) — so any allowlist change belongs in BOTH copies. /config/registration
# is portal-web ONLY. Pointing this at the portal host covers all three.
# =========================================================
ECOSYSTEM_API_BASE_URL=https://portal.apoyolime.com
# ⚠ Must be registered as a NEW, DISTINCT "food-app:<secret>" entry in the
# identity app's ECOSYSTEM_SERVICE_TOKENS (format "<name>:<secret>",
# comma-separated) BEFORE Food's first live ecosystem call. The APP_VERTICAL_SCOPE
# grant added by Slice 3 does nothing without a matching token.
# Never reuse salon-app's or apparel-app's — it would make the apps' calls
# indistinguishable in logs and couple their rotation. Never commit the value.
#
# ⚠⚠ THE VALUE HERE IS THE SECRET ONLY — NOT "food-app:<secret>".
# Confirmed against portal-web's own `authenticateEcosystemCaller` at Slice 13:
# the server splits its ECOSYSTEM_SERVICE_TOKENS entries on the first ":" and
# timing-safe-compares the presented Bearer against the part AFTER it. So the
# identity apps' .env holds `food-app:<secret>` and this file holds `<secret>`
# alone. Getting it wrong is a flat 401 with no hint which half is wrong.
ECOSYSTEM_SERVICE_TOKEN=
# PORTAL_CLAIMS_SERVICE_TOKEN is deliberately NOT listed: Food pushes no
# TimeClaims in any phase of this plan (architecture Part B3). Add it only if a
# genuinely exclusive-time feature ever exists — and note it is a DIFFERENT
# secret from the above despite the similar name (APOYO_ECOSYSTEM.md gotcha).

# =========================================================
# Cross-surface links (Slice 13). Food is ONE build serving TWO origins:
# food.apoyolime.com (buyer) and portal.apoyolime.com/food/... (seller).
# =========================================================
# ⚠ PROD MUST SET THIS. A relative link from the buyer footer into
# /food/onboarding resolves against food.apoyolime.com, where middleware.ts
# 404s the whole /food subtree — so the seller entry point, the one link the
# supply side depends on, would dead-end with nothing in any log. Leave it
# UNSET in local dev: both surfaces answer on localhost:3012 there (middleware's
# unknown-host case) and a relative path is the only thing that works without
# DNS. See lib/links.ts.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SELLER_SURFACE_URL=https://portal.apoyolime.com

# --- Everything below arrives with the slice named beside it. Listed now so the
# --- prod runbook is never assembled from memory. ---

# =========================================================
# Storage & media (Slice 4). Local disk behind lib/storage.ts, shaped so R2 +
# a CDN swaps in as one module later (architecture Part C — R2 is deferred until
# the account exists, deliberately not a Phase-0 dependency).
# =========================================================
# Absolute path to the uploads directory. Unset in dev (defaults to
# <repo>/uploads, already gitignored). ⚠ PROD MUST SET THIS to a path OUTSIDE
# the git checkout, e.g. /home/user/web/food.apoyolime.com/private/uploads — so
# a `git pull`/redeploy can never touch already-written media.
# UPLOADS_BASE_PATH=
MAX_UPLOAD_MB=10
# Public base URL for stored media, read by lib/storage.ts AND by the client-side
# next/image loader (hence NEXT_PUBLIC_). Leave unset for the local-disk driver,
# which serves through this app's own /api/media route. When R2 + a CDN custom
# domain exist, set this to e.g. https://media.apoyolime.com and no component or
# call site changes.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MEDIA_BASE_URL=

# Slice 5 — the shared kap64-translate microservice (VPS-only by its own design;
# confirmed NOT reachable from local dev — see Apparel Slice 5's notes. The
# degrade path is the local default, not a scenario to stage).
# TRANSLATE_SERVICE_URL=http://localhost:5500
# TRANSLATE_API_KEY=

# =========================================================
# Slice 18 — transactional email (architecture Part E6). Port of Salon's own
# SMTP setup (itself ported from the Apoyo-Demia app): prod uses Resend's SMTP
# relay (send-only, no mailbox needed — the order thread handles all in-app
# back-and-forth). SMTP_USER is literally the string "resend"; SMTP_PASS is
# the Resend API key. Unreachable in local dev by default, same posture as
# TRANSLATE_SERVICE_URL above — lib/email.ts's callers are all best-effort and
# never throw back at the caller on a failed send.
# =========================================================
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.resend.com
# SMTP_PORT=465
# SMTP_SECURE=true
# SMTP_USER=resend
# SMTP_PASS=
# SMTP_FROM="Apoyo Food <noreply@apoyolime.com>"

# =========================================================
# Slice 8 — the demo seed (throwaway fixture data, `npm run db:seed:demo`).
# Never set on a database carrying real sellers.
# =========================================================
# Which photo provider the demo seed uses. See prisma/seed-data/photos.ts.
#   mealdb    (default) TheMealDB's free API — real, appetising food photos.
#             ⚠ A development/demo licence, and the images are user-contributed:
#             this is NOT a licence to publish them as real sellers' photos.
#   commons   CC0 / public-domain Wikimedia files pinned in
#             prisma/seed-data/photo-manifest.json. The clean-licence option;
#             the manifest needs human curation before it is usable.
#   synthetic Offline, deterministic, no network and no licence question.
# This is architecture open question 1 and it is the user's call to make.
# SEED_PHOTO_SOURCE=mealdb

# =========================================================
# Slice 9 — demand-event logging (architecture Part E3/E7).
# =========================================================
# ⚠ Salt for the `userIdHash` column. MUST be set in production and MUST stay
# stable across restarts and deploys: if it is unset the process mints a random
# one per boot, which never weakens anonymity but makes the same visitor look
# like a new person after every restart — quietly inflating every distinct-user
# count in the Phase 6 insights. Generate with `openssl rand -hex 24`.
# It is NOT a secret in the credential sense (it authenticates nothing), but
# rotating it discards the ability to correlate history, so treat it as
# write-once.
# DEMAND_HASH_SALT=
